  "findings": [
    {
      "model_finding_id": "F1",
      "proposition": "Advance Flex for broad-audience development, with Plus retained for targeted development. Flex has higher overall appeal than Plus: 60.96% versus 54.01% (+6.95pp; Holm p=.0113). Its purchase-intent lead is inconclusive: 53.22% versus 50.41% (+2.81pp; p=.311).",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/appeal_5",
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/purchase_intent_5",
        "/analysis_claims/1/values/explorers",
        "/analysis_claims/1/values/interactions"
      ],
      "importance": "primary",
      "claim_strength": "Supported overall appeal advantage; no established overall purchase-intent superiority. Advancement is a researcher recommendation.",
      "qualification": "n=700 per concept. Flex–Plus unadjusted 95% intervals are 1.57–12.33pp for appeal and −2.63–8.26pp for intent. Appeal's point estimate exceeds the 5pp materiality threshold, but the interval includes smaller effects. Non-significant intent does not establish equivalence. Broad audience means the eligible meal-kit-open population, not all UK adults.",
      "topic_cluster": "advancement"
    },
    {
      "model_finding_id": "F2",
      "proposition": "Develop Plus specifically for food explorers, who represent 23.83% of the weighted sample. Among explorers, Plus exceeds Flex on appeal (72.12% versus 60.10%; +12.02pp; Holm p=.00665) and intent (63.94% versus 52.40%; +11.54pp; p=.0259). Outside this segment, Flex leads on appeal (61.25% versus 48.42%; +12.83pp; p=.000200), while its intent lead remains inconclusive after correction (53.50% versus 46.23%; +7.27pp; p=.0587).",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "/analysis_claims/1/values/overall_explorer_weighted_pct",
        "/analysis_claims/1/values/explorers",
        "/analysis_claims/1/values/non_explorers",
        "/analysis_claims/1/values/interactions"
      ],
      "importance": "primary",
      "claim_strength": "Pre-specified subgroup evidence supported by direct interaction tests; targeting is a researcher recommendation.",
      "qualification": "Flex/Plus bases are 236/234 explorers and 464/466 non-explorers. Explorer Plus–Flex unadjusted intervals are 3.34–20.69pp for appeal and 2.44–20.64pp for intent; both include effects below 5pp. Non-explorer Flex–Plus intervals are 6.37–19.30pp and 0.73–13.80pp; the latter does not pass Holm correction. Interaction differences are 24.85pp for appeal (95% CI 14.03–35.67; Holm p=.0000203) and 18.81pp for intent (7.60–30.02; p=.00301). Segment share is not an addressable-market estimate.",
      "topic_cluster": "targeting"
    },
    {
      "model_finding_id": "F6",
      "proposition": "Deprioritize Simple in its current form. Its appeal is 41.08% and intent 40.41%; Flex exceeds it by 19.88pp and 12.81pp, and Plus by 12.93pp and 10.00pp after rounding. All four contrasts pass Holm correction (p≤.000576). Easy understanding (92.92%) does not coincide with competitive primary outcomes.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/appeal_5",
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/purchase_intent_5",
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/understanding_5",
        "/analysis_claims/3/values/selected_pct"
      ],
      "importance": "primary",
      "claim_strength": "Supported disadvantages on both primary outcomes; relative development recommendation.",
      "qualification": "n=700 per concept. All four point differences exceed 5pp. Unadjusted intervals are 14.55–25.21pp and 7.42–18.20pp for Flex's appeal and intent advantages, and 7.52–18.34pp and 4.59–15.40pp for Plus's. The last interval includes effects below 5pp. Insufficient differentiation (40.74%) and limited choice (35.44%) are descriptive concerns, not demonstrated causes. No absolute advancement hurdle was supplied.",
      "topic_cluster": "simple_decision"
    },
    {
      "model_finding_id": "F4",
      "proposition": "Improve Plus's clarity and perceived delivery credibility while preserving its differentiation. Plus exceeds Flex on uniqueness (81.39% versus 47.91%), but trails on easy understanding (32.25% versus 95.71%) and credibility (42.15% versus 62.25%). All three contrasts survive Holm correction. Complexity (40.87%) and adventurousness (40.66%) are prominent reported concerns.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/uniqueness_5",
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/understanding_5",
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/credibility_5",
        "/analysis_claims/3/values/selected_pct"
      ],
      "importance": "primary",
      "claim_strength": "Supported rating differences; descriptive development clues and researcher recommendation.",
      "qualification": "n=700 per concept. Plus's uniqueness advantage is 33.48pp (unadjusted 95% CI 28.58–38.39); Flex's understanding and credibility advantages are 63.46pp (59.57–67.35) and 20.10pp (14.79–25.41); each Holm p<.000001. Plus receives difficult-understanding ratings from 32.15%. Likes for new flavours (62.48%), chef inspiration (53.88%) and provenance (48.22%) suggest features to preserve. These diagnostics do not establish causes of intent, actual delivery performance or the effect of a redesign.",
      "topic_cluster": "plus_development"
    },
    {
      "model_finding_id": "F5",
      "proposition": "Do not use Plus's 78.82% premium-conviction score as an overall premium advantage. The question reached only 67.85% of its weighted assigned cell, versus 99.10% for Flex and 99.75% for Simple; their routed scores were 50.09% and 37.73%.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "/analysis_claims/2/values"
      ],
      "importance": "primary",
      "claim_strength": "Descriptive conditional result with a consequential denominator limitation.",
      "qualification": "Routed bases: Plus 483, Flex 694, Simple 698. Eligibility requires post-exposure understanding ≥3. These selected groups do not preserve an unconditional randomized comparison. Unasked responses are unknown, not negative; no overall premium superiority is established.",
      "topic_cluster": "premium_evidence"
    },
    {
      "model_finding_id": "F7",
      "proposition": "Test concrete prices before making commercial commitments. Flex and Plus have numerically similar expected-value scores (56.33% and 56.68%; Flex–Plus −0.35pp; Holm p=.900), while expense concerns are selected by 32.39% and 38.80%, respectively.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "/analysis_claims/0/values/value_5",
        "/analysis_claims/3/values/selected_pct"
      ],
      "importance": "secondary",
      "claim_strength": "Pricing research recommendation; neither a value advantage nor equivalence is established.",
      "qualification": "n=700 per concept. The value-difference unadjusted 95% interval is −5.76–5.07pp. Purchase intent assumes a 'reasonable price' and value an unspecified 'modest premium'; neither measures conversion at a concrete price. Expense percentages were not significance-tested.",
      "topic_cluster": "commercial_qualification"
    }
  ],
  "story": {
    "beats": [
      {
        "order": 1,
        "finding_refs": ["F1", "F6"],
        "headline_intent": "Advance Flex for broad development; deprioritize current Simple",
        "analytical_relationship": "Establish the relative development priorities from primary outcomes, distinguishing Flex's supported appeal advantage from its unresolved overall intent comparison with Plus."
      },
      {
        "order": 2,
        "finding_refs": ["F2"],
        "headline_intent": "Give Plus a focused development audience among food explorers",
        "analytical_relationship": "Qualify the overall decision with the supported reversal in relative appeal and direct evidence of heterogeneity on both primary outcomes."
      },
      {
        "order": 3,
        "finding_refs": ["F4", "F5"],
        "headline_intent": "Preserve Plus's differentiation while addressing clarity and credibility",
        "analytical_relationship": "Translate rating differences and descriptive concerns into development priorities, then explain how understanding-based routing limits the apparent premium advantage."
      },
      {
        "order": 4,
        "finding_refs": ["F7"],
        "headline_intent": "Resolve price-specific demand before commercial commitment",
        "analytical_relationship": "Identify the remaining commercial evidence gap created by hypothetical pricing and reported expense concerns."
      }
    ]
  },
  "method_notes": [
    "This revision selects, ranks and consolidates the supplied verified draft; it introduces no new empirical evidence. Its numerical results and verification record are inherited from that draft, not newly recomputed in this revision.",
    "The study is fictional. Recommendations concern relative concept development within the study and do not validate real market demand or customer behavior.",
    "The verified draft reports 2,100 eligible respondents, 700 per concept, no duplicate IDs, no subgroup-definition or premium-routing mismatches, and 225 structurally unasked premium ratings as the only missing responses.",
    "Percentages use supplied wt_final and valid question-specific bases. Bases are unweighted unless stated otherwise. Concept arrays are ordered Flex, Plus, Simple; five-category distributions run from scores 1 through 5. Top-two-box means 4–5 and bottom-two-box means 1–2. Rounding may produce small discrepancies.",
    "The policy requires Holm correction but does not enumerate exact family membership. The inherited implementation uses all three pairwise concept comparisons within each outcome, separately overall and within each explorer-status group; each primary outcome's three pairwise concept-by-explorer interactions form a separate family. No across-outcome correction is applied. Significance statements are conditional on this implementation, which is not itself documented as pre-specified.",
    "Inherited inference uses independent-cell normal Wald tests with fixed-weight robust proportion variance n/(n−1) × sum[w_i²(y_i−p)²]/sum(w_i)², where y indicates scores 4–5. Difference variances add across independent cells; interaction variances add across four disjoint cells. Calibration-estimation uncertainty is not represented.",
    "All confidence intervals are unadjusted 95% intervals; statistical decisions use Holm-adjusted p-values at .05. Commercial materiality is assessed separately as an absolute top-two-box point difference ≥5pp. A material point estimate does not establish a true effect above 5pp, and non-significance does not establish equivalence.",
    "Food explorer, defined before exposure as mean(P1:P3) ≥4, is the sole pre-specified strategic subgroup. Heterogeneity claims rely on direct interactions, not contrasting subgroup significance levels.",
    "Premium ratings describe only respondents with understanding ≥3. Multiple-response diagnostics are descriptive and may sum above 100%; no causal driver model was fitted.",
    "Evidence references are exact JSON Pointer paths to analysis tables in this response. Stable analysis_id fields identify the inherited analyses. Findings are ordered by research priority; every story finding reference resolves."
  ]
}
